{"id":"d51efc5b66442fea21728c71f9a1fc98ee5fed32","axis":"silent","repo":"dotnet/roslyn","sha":"d51efc5b66442fea21728c71f9a1fc98ee5fed32","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"docs/compilers/CSharp/Compiler","lines":["@@ -49,3 +49,88 @@ ref S replacement = ref s.Self;"," if (condition)","     r = ref replacement;"," ```","+","+## Type parameter inference uses generic constraints","+","+***Introduced in Visual Studio 2026 version 18.14***","+","+In C# 16, method type inference can infer type parameters that occur only in the constraints of other inferred type parameters.","+As a result, a generic method that was previously excluded from overload resolution may become applicable.","+This can change the selected overload, introduce an ambiguity, or produce a more specific diagnostic from the newly applicable candidate.","+Constraint inference can also change type arguments that were already inferable from method arguments.","+","+For example, the following call previously selected the non-generic overload because `U` could not be inferred.","+It now selects the generic overload because `T` is fixed to `string` and `U` is then inferred as `char` from the `IEnumerable<U>` constraint.","+","+```cs","+using System;","+using System.Collections.Generic;","+","+class Program","+{","+    static void M(object value) => Console.WriteLine(\"non-generic\");","+    static void M<T, U>(T value) where T : IEnumerable<U> => Console.WriteLine(\"generic\");","+","+    static void Main()","+    {","+        M(\"test\"); // C# 15: \"non-generic\"; C# 16: \"generic\"","+    }","+}","+```","+","+To preserve the previous overload selection, explicitly convert the argument to the intended parameter type:","+","+```cs","+M((object)\"test\"); // \"non-generic\"","+```","+","+Even when there is only one method and its type arguments were already inferable, constraint inference can change the inferred types.","+This can change behavior or cause a previously valid call to fail a constraint check.","+For example:","+","+```cs","+using System;","+","+static Type M<T, U>(T t, U u) where T : I<U> where U : B => typeof(U);","+","+Console.WriteLine(M(new Holder(), new B()).Name); // C# 15: \"B\"; C# 16: CS0311","+","+interface I<in T> { }","+class A { }","+class B : A { }","+class Holder : I<A> { }","+```","+","+Previously, `U` was inferred as `B` from the second argument.","+It is now inferred as `A` after inference through the contravariant `I<U>` constraint, violating the `where U : B` constraint.","+To preserve the previous type arguments, specify them explicitly:","+","+```cs","+Console.WriteLine(M<Holder, B>(new Holder(), new B()).Name); // \"B\"","+```","+","+The inferred types can also change without producing a diagnostic, including when a type argument is inferred from a lambda's return type:","+","+```cs","+using System;","+","+static Type M<T, U, V>(T t, V v, Func<V, U> f) where T : I<U> => typeof(U);","+","+Console.WriteLine(M(new Holder(), 0, x => new B()).Name); // C# 15: \"B\"; C# 16: \"A\"","+","+interface I<in T> { }","+class A { }","+class B : A { }","+class Holder : I<A> { }","+```","+","+Previously, `U` was inferred as `B` from the lambda's return type.","+Constraint inference now contributes `A` through the `where T : I<U>` constraint, and `U` is inferred as `A`.","+The lambda can still return a `B`, so the call remains valid, but the method executes with a different type argument.","+To preserve the previous behavior, specify the type arguments explicitly:","+","+```cs","+Console.WriteLine(M<Holder, B, int>(new Holder(), 0, x => new B()).Name); // \"B\"","+```","+","+See [the type parameter inference from constraints proposal](https://github.com/dotnet/csharplang/issues/9453)."],"additions":85,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Binder/Binder_Conversions.cs","lines":["@@ -2115,9 +2115,9 @@ static ImmutableArray<MethodSymbol> filterOutBadGenericMethods(","                                     argToParamMap: default,","